ations II and III are first-order ordinary differential equations." :answer/id #uuid "6a781e2b-45dc-581c-b89d-91bc1aa2c572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408177} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e3890ce-bcc5-5fd3-810f-d1691316f3b1" :answer-field/key "None" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20db6c27-e8a9-50d0-bb54-61baf97c40e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-96676" :question/problem "Which of the following are first-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - $3{y}^{''}+2x{y}^{'}={x}^{2}y$\nII. - $5{x}^{2}y+3{y}^{5}{y}^{'}=4x$\nIII. - $\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{y}^{'}}+7{x}^{3}={e}^{y}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is of first-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{''},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\n- Equation III is also a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] ["Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166813 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "85cea071-187a-5be3-b347-b4aa26aead57" :question/math-academy-id "q-96680" :question/problem "Which of the following are first-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - $3{y}^{'}+2x{y}^{''}={x}^{2}y$\nII. - $5{x}^{2}{y}^{‴}+3x{y}^{'}={e}^{x}$\nIII. - $4{({y}^{'})}^{3}+7{x}^{3}=x({y}^{2}+{({y}^{'})}^{2})$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is of first-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{''},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] ["Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166813 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ecd7d48-e192-56ba-a9eb-6c4280135677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ecd7d48-e192-56ba-a9eb-6c4280135677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234667} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1bfa42a-8d4e-593c-aa9b-c50e784fddb2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "615b21ea-17f4-5ce5-8433-316c3df76ca0" :question/math-academy-id "q-167787" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}-x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x)\n$$\n\nThe order of this differential equation is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "We have the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}-x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ which has order $4.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $4.$"}] ["Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166813 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22ac80f9-d1a9-57b5-8a75-ee4e690886f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250849} {:answer/id #uuid "254a46ec-eea5-5a9e-b90d-ccb407bc95b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250874} {:answer/id #uuid "625479f9-bef7-5c1a-937a-158ca732f7fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :ans