}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] ["Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50433ce6-5f47-50dd-9e9b-85d17224a715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358441} {:answer/id #uuid "7227653e-d62c-513d-9a69-bdac1666e341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358748} {:answer/id #uuid "b29e28c8-4bf6-5d40-8c9d-8c40c5abc4ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359305} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c5eecb-38a5-5274-a5bd-d4d7a88d8192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359362} {:answer/id #uuid "dbea906c-0509-5763-971e-c278d2b7e4c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b29e28c8-4bf6-5d40-8c9d-8c40c5abc4ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359305} :answer-field/id #uuid "c723b3f2-2a27-58ff-a975-f834b8df18f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e9b8880-609b-5a27-b50e-083098d2cbea" :question/math-academy-id "q-95984" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{y}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0$\n- $2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=\\sqrt{x}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3y.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] ["Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16e4213a-6395-5967-b49c-6c3117d1903a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357951} {:answer/id #uuid "28ac1ff9-fc68-573d-a3d5-403823814000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358121} {:answer/id #uuid "5073e9de-3703-5784-a800-8126b1bc76e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358443} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c9f202-6f38-53fd-9ca3-db0700ade3a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359308} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc470f3-0209-52a0-bfb9-3961cf6547e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16e4213a-6395-5967-b49c-6c3117d1903a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357951} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6e83435-c76c-57e4-9003-7b26a38e34f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ca3e21f-6e14-592d-bc53-233d6a2ad52a" :question/math-academy-id "q-96387" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{y}=0$\n- $\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}-xy={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{y} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{3}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}-xy & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′} & =xy+{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2(xy+{e}^{x}) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=2(xy+{e}^{x}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] ["Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98fb69b1-0fe2-5868-8c3d-537e50a23e41" :question/math-academy-id "q-118863" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - ${y}^{'}-8{e}^{y}=2$\nII. - $y{y}^{'}+3=\\operatorname{cos}y$\nIII. - $x{y}^{'}-8={e}^{y}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}-8{e}^{y} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =8{e}^{y}+2 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=8{e}^{y}+2.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{'}+3 & =\\operatorname{cos}y \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}y-3}{y} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}y-3}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{'}-8 & ={e}^{y} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\frac{{e}^{y}+8}{x} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{y}+8}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] ["Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cb765ab-56a4-5072-853c-55e0a9cdf71b" :question/math-academy-id "q-96702" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x={e}^{y}$\nII. - $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2=\\sqrt{y}$\nIII. - $4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{x}{y}=5y$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x & ={e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{y}+x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={e}^{y}+x.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2 & =\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{y}+2}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{y}+2}{2}.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{x}{y} & =5y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{4}(5y+\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{4}(5y+\\frac{x}{y}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] ["Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ca7da4a-7115-52ea-963d-df4449aa6da2" :question/math-academy-id "q-96703" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - $x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3}=2$\nII. - $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-7{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{y}$\nIII. - $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{ln}y$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2+3{y}^{3}}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{2+3{y}^{3}}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-7{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =7{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=7{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{ln}y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] ["Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ea5819d-4667-5352-b5c8-80e2834cf13a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358018} {:answer/id #uuid "59be33c0-a3eb-50cd-80cc-b5e5fe38b29d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358531} {:answer/id #uuid "95c04dd3-060f-5893-86c4-859642fc3780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359062} {:answer/id #uuid "a54c97dd-6fab-54e5-a56a-2142b0f5d4f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359186} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e78e47-eb49-591d-b336-f1247069acc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9e78e47-eb49-591d-b336-f1247069acc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359524} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e8a452d-8956-5c9a-aa11-e72561f0e970" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "483de092-6fe5-5b9f-85ea-10c713e1d18a" :question/math-academy-id "q-96705" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3}=0$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$\n- $5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x}=0$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2x.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] ["Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad324671-5207-59c5-b2d9-048cb88806c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-96712" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - ${y}^{'}=1-\\sqrt{y}$\nII. - ${y}^{'}-\\operatorname{tan}y={e}^{2}$\nIII. - $x{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{y}=4$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=1-\\sqrt{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}-\\operatorname{tan}y & ={e}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\operatorname{tan}y+{e}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\operatorname{tan}y+{e}^{2}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{y} & =4 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\frac{4y+1}{xy} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{4y+1}{xy}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] ["Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "305095fe-581b-5daa-a454-6a748f03800a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250964} {:answer/id #uuid "4183e9b8-94a7-58ac-9371-c8bc7093f14b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251097} {:answer/id #uuid "a133aeb6-ceff-5917-b87e-69ceb7df5725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251944} {:answer/id #uuid "b21060d2-6b4a-56cb-96fc-3bd5135aca49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252110} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ef4aa2-68f4-562e-a596-0b3f9dd022bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b21060d2-6b4a-56cb-96fc-3bd5135aca49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252110} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf8ecce3-7eff-58e1-9e5c-0b494fc4b8f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4fb73e0-2435-50c4-b136-c8ed7ccb224f" :question/math-academy-id "q-118858" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${e}^{2y}{y}^{′}+1=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+\\sqrt{xy}=2$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2y}{y}^{′}+1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-{e}^{-2y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-{e}^{-2y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{xy} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2-\\sqrt{xy} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=2-\\sqrt{xy}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] ["Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cd93834-0277-5387-aef8-9fb2b2dca832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250662} {:answer/id #uuid "2dfe8bb8-9fe4-58e9-9357-d9810cfd1c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250947} {:answer/id #uuid "5915dc66-227f-5ffa-a6dc-7aa1d5c0003c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251286} {:answer/id #uuid "89de18c4-f123-5cfb-a333-0c7f91f1df60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251713} {:answer/id #uuid "ae98fe96-9458-566e-83e7-67fa2456bcf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89de18c4-f123-5cfb-a333-0c7f91f1df60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251713} :answer-field/id #uuid "89cbcfab-b32e-520a-b3ab-3f4a67487783" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81da2b48-aa38-5eb5-8b7d-567a07263315" :question/math-academy-id "q-118859" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-3{x}^{2}=1$\n- ${y}^{′}+6y={e}^{3y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-3{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+1.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+6y & ={e}^{3y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={e}^{3y}-6y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={e}^{3y}-6y.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] ["Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d03b5cd-402b-5e9e-9a18-d69abf0fecef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250665} {:answer/id #uuid "9afcaa3d-c1de-5df2-a5ef-339b9e0a7f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251885} {:answer/id #uuid "9d395e94-386c-5819-87a8-8bda90430144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251909} {:answer/id #uuid "dcdca02e-8c4d-540e-84e2-e8367000e61a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252498} {:answer/id #uuid "e82d2c6d-1cbb-594d-b6d8-774352ea5c82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d395e94-386c-5819-87a8-8bda90430144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251909} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b734866-2c74-5ecd-a881-13ac9fe9cb09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40dc1226-9a4e-5dff-a14a-c81909c8002d" :question/math-academy-id "q-118868" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{′}+6x{y}^{2}=8$\n- ${y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x}=3{y}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({y}^{2}+2)$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+6x{y}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{8-6x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{8-6x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x} & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =3{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=3{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({y}^{2}+2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] ["Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06cb9eed-b128-5baf-a6ef-6264db0751b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250607} {:answer/id #uuid "166ce4d7-7cb0-5417-9912-96973353838a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250745} {:answer/id #uuid "23b62090-f1be-5651-855a-e9e83de03d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250860} {:answer/id #uuid "332486c7-fce9-5267-827f-8efc6c8ebcd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250982} {:answer/id #uuid "d324f459-0823-529b-9901-a4f4bef19942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d324f459-0823-529b-9901-a4f4bef19942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252422} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c4b5e39-6b12-5c74-8707-65e7e8a70568" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1c949f8-b9ab-5207-8749-bf7052ce14f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-118869" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi }=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+{y}^{2}={x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi } & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi } \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi }.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] ["Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16f980f2-1577-5537-be57-943a8ca18c14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250752} {:answer/id #uuid "5559e3f6-f3c1-51db-b891-67bcbea95dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251252} {:answer/id #uuid "58f44adc-fe68-5d69-a276-6b4ee99d3987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251284} {:answer/id #uuid "80518909-a16e-5896-bc3a-0a5ebcf2ed97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251633} {:answer/id #uuid "dea23c9f-51f5-52ab-a86a-5e2aae041f93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58f44adc-fe68-5d69-a276-6b4ee99d3987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251284} :answer-field/id #uuid "a970fe1e-cf80-5fbc-a811-06ed3857f0bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e075483-5dc5-5bbb-be2d-e2d1164eea57" :question/math-academy-id "q-118870" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={e}^{3-y}-2$\n- $y{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=1$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+y)$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{3-y}-2.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] ["Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54cf01e5-ceba-59ae-b9ce-961e6fa63f1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358485} {:answer/id #uuid "6642d55d-578c-5eb5-acb5-736fb593558e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358645} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab571c7-f4b9-5fc6-b56d-7b725d4ac0e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358684} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a9b1d7-4195-562c-9576-a87690eb06cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359610} {:answer/id #uuid "e52cfd81-732f-5366-8b59-942dce48aad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e52cfd81-732f-5366-8b59-942dce48aad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359782} :answer-field/id #uuid "418c151a-65ae-536f-b50c-e43314ab52fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5388ae42-2792-5587-8853-f61a559c11a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-96711" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}=1$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y+{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{e}^{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] ["Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fe058d7-ca8f-5ec9-a8e2-9fd5a771d327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358868} {:answer/id #uuid "82553e5d-4700-5e54-a1c2-0d13d8cef8d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358894} {:answer/id #uuid "82e32d06-06ec-51c4-8f94-ee4325173f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358903} {:answer/id #uuid "ae372e17-bae3-5de3-8999-65c43a2f73be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359262} {:answer/id #uuid "ef26e56a-6ff5-5d66-a47f-969e15992152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82553e5d-4700-5e54-a1c2-0d13d8cef8d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358894} :answer-field/id #uuid "34de3c2c-bd2e-5f37-8e74-58bcd45e4f01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83ac0667-a1bc-5ca7-903e-f359054c0435" :question/math-academy-id "q-96713" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${e}^{-y}{y}^{′}=-\\sqrt{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}-{y}^{3}=2{e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}=3\\sqrt{y}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y}{y}^{′} & =-\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\sqrt{y}{e}^{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-\\sqrt{y}{e}^{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{y}^{3} & =2{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={y}^{3}+2{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={y}^{3}+2{e}^{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}} & =3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] ["Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97413256-ae38-5ca1-af59-b379fcddf6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359076} {:answer/id #uuid "be61f1aa-715c-58a9-bf35-b6c175cdcca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359414} {:answer/id #uuid "d831e2fa-6936-5e03-b28e-da5770d67193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359645} {:answer/id #uuid "e538c28e-030c-5096-97f5-1c88b11a094c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359783} {:answer/id #uuid "eb355239-fadb-56b3-a002-9305b1755b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97413256-ae38-5ca1-af59-b379fcddf6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359076} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5c91399-0edf-587d-9fdd-3f95d90ee82c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb1a3b53-c6cd-51f3-bcd2-63da1f7e9756" :question/math-academy-id "q-96715" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\sqrt{2y}$\n- ${y}^{′}-{y}^{3}=3\\sqrt{y}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\sqrt{2y}.$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{y}^{3} & =3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={y}^{3}+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={y}^{3}+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] ["Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c281305-07f6-5248-addb-56dbffcf43d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357994} {:answer/id #uuid "425002f6-cd94-5adb-aa30-e3bf1d1a9152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358317} {:answer/id #uuid "4a9d1e4b-41f4-5c1d-9dcf-116a15f9cba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358390} {:answer/id #uuid "ebce119b-c42b-5cf6-9cf8-67a9131342b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359850} {:answer/id #uuid "ef95271b-65fd-5af5-a274-1a33d8399734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef95271b-65fd-5af5-a274-1a33d8399734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359886} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbcb2446-5499-5729-916a-7f9000a58ab7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c01fd7ca-ed83-5e17-8036-cd03a49c52f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-95985" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $2x{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=1$\n- ${y}^{′}-2xy={e}^{y}$\n- $2x{y}^{″}={y}^{′},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2x{y}^{′}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2x{y}^{″}={y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] ["Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0497b675-c4d7-5aec-bf1c-5d24dee89d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357794} {:answer/id #uuid "13713ac4-10e1-59ec-a786-cc04cf0b96f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357917} {:answer/id #uuid "23faf99c-a7e1-552f-8309-c422f0cf6b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358072} {:answer/id #uuid "732f6afd-9270-562c-a749-43bd8024a8e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358753} {:answer/id #uuid "78ed70b7-8f6a-5447-93db-c6e70c7ee75f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78ed70b7-8f6a-5447-93db-c6e70c7ee75f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358800} :answer-field/id #uuid "da428f89-b735-5f26-9767-c7c21805d046" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed0b0a68-7ad2-55d7-8d80-82f9a5c1ced6" :question/math-academy-id "q-96986" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′},\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$\n- $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=2$\n- $4{y}^{′}-\\frac{1}{3}xy=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] ["Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72a71df0-c1f3-5b09-87e2-03d28dcff804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251497} {:answer/id #uuid "7a0a569c-63ca-58be-b58a-82f10a235728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251569} {:answer/id #uuid "92613281-1dd4-5bc5-9d74-331121e7cd48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251805} {:answer/id #uuid "a23e2128-2cb2-5e2b-bd36-5cda47badacf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251951} {:answer/id #uuid "b39f6185-b1d3-5317-8c72-41a88ba25ea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72a71df0-c1f3-5b09-87e2-03d28dcff804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251497} :answer-field/id #uuid "97b7d650-0be2-5452-8b3f-6b34fa085297" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7775ce93-f97a-564e-ab19-6ca38ffa6f3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-118786" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{′}=8y,\\,y(-1)=3$\n- $8{y}^{′}-xy=3$\n- $3{y}^{″}=4y,\\,y(-1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(-1)=1$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{′}=8y$ together with the initial condition $y(-1)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $3{y}^{″}=4y$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(-1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(-1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] ["Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85607d47-df50-5e10-bf9d-699a7a42f32f" :question/math-academy-id "q-96987" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nI. - ${x}^{2}{y}^{''}-y=2x,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{'}(1)=3$\nII. - $x{y}^{‴}-{e}^{x}{y}^{'}=9y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{'}(0)=1,\\,{y}^{''}(0)=2$\nIII. - $2{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{x}={e}^{y},\\,y(0)=1$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{''}-y=2x$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{'}(1)=3.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation $x{y}^{‴}-{e}^{x}{y}^{'}=9y$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{'}(0)=1,$ and ${y}^{''}(0)=2.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{x}={e}^{y}$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] ["Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06aa2a7f-30bd-5a01-b36a-d58cb045b822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357812} {:answer/id #uuid "44833b4e-8024-5de6-a7ca-882b1d937246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358338} {:answer/id #uuid "90d56596-3f6d-592e-b3c2-42c9dc40f255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359020} {:answer/id #uuid "d761052c-063a-5a98-b283-80aad4e6e472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359638} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c5b0ef-ed68-5bb8-8ff4-af6cdde568a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d761052c-063a-5a98-b283-80aad4e6e472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359638} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fa8d7e2-d39a-58a0-9902-3d32f92ecf53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e83e1ae-37f6-5806-9569-e87bd10cd10f" :question/math-academy-id "q-96992" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $3{x}^{2}{y}^{′}={y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1$\n- $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y,\\,y(1)=2$\n- ${y}^{′}-x\\sqrt{y}={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{x}^{2}{y}^{′}={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\n- System III  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] ["Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "261fe41f-66fd-591a-9d3e-09ed463bfcc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250881} {:answer/id #uuid "467daa96-9189-5add-9f3c-afcd2ec9af56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251139} {:answer/id #uuid "5ab3bd1f-f80d-5a1d-a7cc-7a11dd1348c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251300} {:answer/id #uuid "9563760a-6dd1-5c49-a006-6fda975970b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251836} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d34e53-59e0-5e62-b65a-58860e6376cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9563760a-6dd1-5c49-a006-6fda975970b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251836} :answer-field/id #uuid "71f2be7e-50e6-547b-bb91-282d9b2201d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4247b2af-dd08-543e-82b1-e9616731f453" :question/math-academy-id "q-118787" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-x=-7y,\\,y(2)=3$\n- ${x}^{4}{y}^{‴}+3y={2}^{y},\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{″}(0)=0$\n- $x{y}^{″}-6y=14,\\,y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}-x=-7y$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${x}^{4}{y}^{‴}+3y={2}^{y}$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1,$ and ${y}^{″}(0)=0.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $x{y}^{″}-6y=14$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] ["Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bd7c5a7-2f2a-57f8-8b68-c8b306fe8766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251181} {:answer/id #uuid "7d173ac7-d801-5343-af8c-422d15c088b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251596} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5a853a-1dd7-52c9-9713-a51aa63256be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251721} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e855b2-2a0e-5a63-ade3-f0cbb4a0eccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252539} {:answer/id #uuid "f2bb6e0c-cd51-510d-907f-42ebc72626a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2bb6e0c-cd51-510d-907f-42ebc72626a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252679} :answer-field/id #uuid "da681ee5-4026-5d18-8cbb-0aee21aa73df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "941e9c2c-1b18-5669-bb2b-964e53cd46ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-118788" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{‴}+9{x}^{3}{y}^{′}=-7y,\\,y(0)={y}^{′}(0)={y}^{″}(0)=-4$\n- $x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(0)=2$\n- $6{y}^{″}+xy={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{‴}+9{x}^{3}{y}^{′}=-7y$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)={y}^{′}(0)={y}^{″}(0)=-4.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] ["Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05cf027e-9ac2-50c5-913b-013f09edb627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250599} {:answer/id #uuid "62c59880-1838-53fa-8ca2-e7dff4e739af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251360} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca9699d-f2d9-5397-b88c-0708539b80b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251904} {:answer/id #uuid "b4098680-b7c4-548d-b62f-2f2a8e17d139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252132} {:answer/id #uuid "d21368d8-4d18-5863-94e3-6b48e3c36458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05cf027e-9ac2-50c5-913b-013f09edb627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250599} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fc3a219-4bfd-592c-aaa9-7e29c36cd584" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c7ca5ad-bcbb-5515-be28-446ac5363a18" :question/math-academy-id "q-118790" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-9x,\\,y(1)=-4$\n- ${y}^{′}-8{x}^{3}=-y,\\,y(0)=-3$\n- ${y}^{″}+y=3$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}=-9x$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=-4.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}-8{x}^{3}=-y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=-3.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] ["Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253515 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f629dcb-deb6-5bc1-9542-ee7019ade83e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250957} {:answer/id #uuid "80eee9e7-e958-54c7-8dfc-8162423b5453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251639} {:answer/id #uuid "864ac549-5ce9-5d7f-8cc0-9f8450323894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251679} {:answer/id #uuid "86d3cd9d-e46f-567b-ac48-c0f8aa1bff7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251687} {:answer/id #uuid "be15148e-6a47-56a8-a00d-50fc49ad5b04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "864ac549-5ce9-5d7f-8cc0-9f8450323894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251679} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7b864dc-f379-5dd7-b330-e19f03ce5c45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7204e641-7358-50b0-b9c2-d492a160bc98" :question/math-academy-id "q-118791" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2$\n- ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x},\\,y(1)=6$\n- $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(4)=-3$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6$ together with the initial condition $y(4)=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] ["Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f43340e-14ba-55ef-be8c-ef1cd787a6eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358023} {:answer/id #uuid "718accdf-d9a3-5253-98aa-ae4903fcac71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358742} {:answer/id #uuid "899e4d94-377f-5a14-922b-59f810bd8af2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358953} {:answer/id #uuid "caa166e3-8953-5fbc-9ae6-7bea61dc8ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359528} {:answer/id #uuid "ff922e84-d0b6-57f4-ab37-48e970cb34ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f43340e-14ba-55ef-be8c-ef1cd787a6eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358023} :answer-field/id #uuid "845a686c-6a12-5b80-92f3-19ed1c6b30c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "765ad5be-eea7-5159-b982-461fb59d4587" :question/math-academy-id "q-96988" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $2x-{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\n- $3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{x}y={y}^{2},\\,y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=1$\n- $2x{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}={y}^{′},\\,y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{x}y={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=1.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2x{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}={y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] ["Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360738 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23264a8b-8df6-5874-a144-ca68b4cbe298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358060} {:answer/id #uuid "7784ce64-03ae-5ec8-876c-629fc45c3fda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358782} {:answer/id #uuid "96bee514-2c6a-5789-9365-eecb29e4f851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359071} {:answer/id #uuid "d7df7b82-4423-51d2-9b32-202d87afdaa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359642} {:answer/id #uuid "e388476a-3c99-5aab-ac42-0c26b86cc367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7784ce64-03ae-5ec8-876c-629fc45c3fda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358782} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fb9ff27-ec8c-5a4e-bba3-09983f1442df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a63b835-35e3-55e6-8b41-bbb1ab5d0792" :question/math-academy-id "q-96990" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${x}^{3}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,y(1)=1$\n- $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{‴}-x{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{4},\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2,\\,{y}^{″}(0)=1$\n- $\\sqrt{3}{y}^{′}-3x=2y,\\,y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2)=1$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${x}^{3}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=1.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{‴}-x{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{4}$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2,$ and ${y}^{″}(0)=1.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\sqrt{3}{y}^{′}-3x=2y$ together with the initial condition $y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2)=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}]}
